WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.666
Hi, I'm Marlo!

00:00:02.666 --> 00:00:06.491
Seven charges on a sphere settle into a pentagonal bipyramid, and ten

00:00:06.491 --> 00:00:08.404
agents wrote a proof of it.

00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:13.633
The idea: slice the problem by the closest pair of points, and

00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:16.407
give each slice a certificate the kernel checks exactly.

00:00:19.175 --> 00:00:23.116
Take any seven distinct points, and find the two that are closest to opposite.

00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:27.063
If no pair is nearly opposite, one bound settles the whole case.

00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:30.419
If a pair is nearly opposite, cover that region with five

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:31.487
slabs and a cap.

00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:35.719
Each slice gets a certificate built from positive matrices and a lower

00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:36.990
bound on the energy.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.438
The Lean kernel checks every certificate as exact integer arithmetic.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:45.426
The energy is just a sum of inverse distances.

00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:48.862
Each pair adds one over the distance between the two points.

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:51.477
Sum that over all twenty-one pairs of points.

00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:56.970
On the left, a cap and five slabs cover the nearly-opposite region.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.994
On the right, a single case covers everything else, with room to spare.

00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:05.632
The answer's energy is fourteen point four five, and every certificate

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:06.874
has to beat it.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:13.877
A second, independent kernel re-checked all forty-seven thousand declarations, with no errors.

00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:19.758
Six slices fall to a certificate; only the slice holding the bipyramid

00:01:19.758 --> 00:01:21.172
needs the hard argument.

00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:26.646
To recap: split on the closest pair, bound each slice, and let

00:01:26.646 --> 00:01:28.353
two kernels check the whole thing.

00:01:28.773 --> 00:01:30.786
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:30.786 --> 00:01:31.361
I'm Marlo.

00:01:31.361 --> 00:01:31.649
Bye!
